  "84d032f6-566b-436c-aa53-9941cc0b4b73": {
   "kind": "theory-wording",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p14-formula-block",
     "nodeId": "p14-formula-definition",
     "replacement": "**Midpoint** is the point halfway between the two endpoints of an interval.\n\nMidpoint: https://www.geogebra.org/m/uepqnx7k\n\nThe **mean** (average) of two numbers $a$ and $b$ is $\\text{mean}=\\frac{a+b}{2}$. The midpoint has the mean of the endpoints' $x$-coordinates and the mean of their $y$-coordinates.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}M&=(\\text{Mean of }x,\\text{Mean of }y)\\\\&=(\\bar{x},\\bar{y})\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo find the midpoint of an interval:\n\n1. Find $x$ midpoint, $\\bar{x}$, the mean of the $x$-coordinate endpoints.\n2. Find $y$ midpoint, $\\bar{y}$, the mean of the $y$-coordinate endpoints.\n3. The midpoint is $M=(\\bar{x},\\bar{y})$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p14-formula-block",
     "targetFingerprint": "a97b3b6c86762956715b71d3afc78fcd43e7c711176ba42607e654d33c24e013",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/coordinate-geometry-v1/20261109-094213",
   "revision": 195
