  "3d7f5ed6-ac89-4946-b845-4d1fcd90cf81": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p18-distance-formula",
     "nodeId": "p18-distance-definition-example",
     "replacement": "Distance: https://www.geogebra.org/m/t94gke48\n\nThe **distance** between two points is the length of the interval joining them. This interval is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle whose other two sides are the rise and the run, so **Pythagoras' theorem** gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}d^{2}&=\\text{rise}^{2}+\\text{run}^{2}\\\\d&=\\sqrt{\\text{rise}^{2}+\\text{run}^{2}}\\end{aligned}$$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p18-distance-formula",
